WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.709 --> 00:00:10.229
Comparison is where course thinking gets real. This page holds two or three sources next to each other so you can see what each one argues.

2
00:00:10.229 --> 00:00:21.237
Pick two or three sources. Use the picker on the right. Readings, videos, and audio can sit together, so choose sources that speak to the same question from different angles.

3
00:00:21.237 --> 00:00:29.900
Read the side-by-side panels. Each panel keeps the source's core idea, its evidence type, and what it does not prove visible.

4
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:40.115
Press Synthesize when you are ready. The synthesis builds a frame across your chosen sources. Use it as a thinking scaffold, not as sentences to copy.

5
00:00:40.115 --> 00:00:47.658
Save, copy, or print the synthesis. Save to my notes keeps it on this device so you can return to it later.

6
00:00:47.658 --> 00:00:57.054
Write your own take. One or two sentences in your own words are worth more than the whole generated frame. Put them in your weekly notes.

7
00:00:57.054 --> 00:01:02.297
Comparisons are never graded. They exist to sharpen your written work.

8
00:01:02.297 --> 00:01:09.028
Where next: Carry your comparison insight into this week's reflection or your next assignment.
